    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A knowledge of the average pressure is necessary for reservoir performance analyses and predictions. A common method to evaluate average pressure is pressure build-up analysis. The theory of pressure build-up analysis assumes at least an approximate knowledge of the drainage area of the well which has been shut-in. If a reservoir is closed, the drainage area of a well is proportional to its production rate in pseudo steady-state. In general, the drainage area has a simple regular shape and can be estimated with reasonable accuracy.When a part of the reservoir boundary is contacted by an active aquifer, the well drainage areas are very much different, both in shape and size, from those obtained for closed boundary reservoirs. The average pressure calculated for water-drive reservoirs using closed boundary drainage areas may be seriously in error. Very little work has been done to evaluate drainage areas in water-drive reservoirs. This study was carried out to contribute to the small fund of information on this subject.The reservoir with a part of the boundary at constant pressure is simulated by using the principle of superposition. The line source solution for a constant rate production well in an infinite reservoir is used. Equations describing the movement of a fluid particle under steady and unsteady states of flow are derived from the superposed line source solutions. A finite difference scheme is used to trace the movement of a particle. The limiting streamlines are used to delineate the drainage boundaries. Another model to trace the water-oil front movement was developed using the streamline approach.The drainage areas in the simple water-drive systems are studied as a function of production time and the production rate ratio of the wells in the system. For very short production times, the drainage areas are shared equally for equal rate wells in the systems studied. The behavior of the drainage area as a function of the production time is related to the pressure drawdown behavior of the well. The three stages shown by a drawdown graph, i.e . (1) semi-log, (2) transitional and (3) steady-state are also evident on a drainage area vs. production time graph.It is concluded on the basis of this study that a complete or a partial water-drive reservoir containing a large number of wells can be viewed as a group of simple units of symmetry. The drainage areas of the wells in the simple unit of symmetry are proportional to the actual drainage areas of the corresponding wells in the system considered as a whole or as a composite of the units. Thus, it is possible to study a large and complex reservoir with the help of a complete knowledge of the drainage area of wells in reservoirs of simple geometric shapes.",
